				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tblackburn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1496792); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1496792&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="TBlackburn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1496792)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tblackburn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1496792); return false;" >TBlackburn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423475245 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Feb 2015 09:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2228682">
									

<p>As someone with a ton of write-ups on their hard-disk and hardly any posted (not even on the sandbox page), I think this is a much needed notification.</p>
<p>To note&#8230; the first time I tried my hand at making an SCP, I offered it up to the irc-chat channel for review. I was pretty much ridiculed out of the channel. There was no critique offered, nor real help on how to improve. It was pretty much a room full of people making fun of me, filling in their most ridiculous ad-libs for the redacted parts to try and tell me it was an unneccesary or unclear redaction and whatnot.</p>
<p>When I finally thought I had a halfway decent idea again, I remade me a sandbox page and put the link up on the forum. And again I was pretty much booted out on my ass. Of all the people who replied to tell me my idea sucked, only ~one~ person offered proper enough feedback with which I (reluctantly) had to agree.</p>
<p>But comparing the responses I got with the those a lot of new faces got on the 'most recently created' page. And those set against the ones a lot of the 'older and established' writers got on their work, made me feel that for a lot of people it's just a knee-jerk response to automatically not like something not written by 'awesomeauthorX'.</p>
<p>I understand, and realize, that quality control is important. And that there also needs to be a way to prevent anyone just slapping something they saw in a movie or series into the archive. But it would do a lot of people a lot of good to also realize that if you crack down unneccesarily hard on newbies dipping their toes into the water, that they'll leave and the community itself will just stagnate.</p>
<p>That's why I think this write-up is a good one. If I know I can expect hard, but fair, critique with feedback on why something needs improving and how to best tackle it, I will be more at ease with actually putting my work forwards.<br />
On the other hand, if I'm going to get a parade of people just going 'this sucks' or 'this does not conform to my personal tastes' without actually explaining the what, how and why of it, I would just end up keeping all my ideas to myself again.</p>
<p>As for my actual future plans&#8230; I recently awoke remembering something I dreamed about. I've not actually retained memories of a dream for years now. And the thing I actually remembered was the description and effects of an anomalous item&#8230;<br />
I'd say that in itself is already pretty anomalous.<br />
I'm just going to write it out as practice and toss it to the lions once again&#8230; until I actually improve enough to feel I can submit something to the frontpage.</p>

							</div>

								
					
