<p>Yeah, no.<br />
While there's indeed terribad criticism on the forums, chat has ,at least in my time, been the single best place to get feedback in.</p>
<p>I think what your problem is that you came in there with a done write-up of a terrible idea , which links to what Kalinin said about correctly identifying the problem. To use an analogy, if I'm presented with a roast pig that hasn't been gutted, I won't go on a tirade on how the skin is full of blisters because they have salted it through the baking - it is a fault in the means of preparation, but the key problem here is that the thing is full of shit. In the same way, lingering on ways an article with a terrible central idea fails in various technical aspects is wasting everyone's time, and giving the false impression that if you fix those, it will work.</p>
<p>For the future, I recommend you show up in chat, and actually brainstorm there, and bounce whatever rudimentary ideas you might have out on people there. This especially works for newbies because when you're new, you need a strong idea as your execution is likely gonna be OK at best, and usually ,that sort of idea can be identified upfront.</p>

							</div>

								
					
