														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2228702">
									

<p>Well, in that case, would it not have been more useful to just say &quot;Yeah, this idea is pretty bad.&quot; rather than to have a bunch of people play the ad-lib game with &quot;[redacted]&quot;?<br />
It's a difference between telling the cook they should go back to the beginning and get a properly prepared pig and taking out the viscera of the roast in front of you and playing puppetry with it in front of the cook and the rest of the patrons.</p>
<p>And yes, it was a pretty terribad idea. I realized that when I came back to it a little while later and re-read it, shook the concept a few times and just heard it rattle on all sides. I'll not waste any time and pixels on even remotely defending it.</p>
<p>It is just a pretty intimidating thing to step into a community where everyone is already so integrated with one another and that presents a continuingly more inflexible set of criteria to uphold. And that was about three years ago.</p>
<p>I concede that maybe I came in with my newness at an awkward time. Back in the day when a different community I was in flourished there were moments, usually late at night, when the chat devolved in a giant pile of silly. And if someone new popped in, an admin had to catch them quickly before the nonsense drove them off.<br />
Perhaps there was already such a mood going, and I just provided more fodder. It still made me feel like I was pretty much just tarred and feathered and set on a rail to laugh at.</p>
<p>While that may have been a personal experience, it's still an experience that a response like &quot;Yeah, no.&quot; won't quite dispell.</p>

							</div>

								
					
