<blockquote>
<p>Well, in that case, would it not have been more useful to just say &quot;Yeah, this idea is pretty bad.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As much as I knew the chat back in my day, people probably did point that out. Things might have changed though.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>rather than to have a bunch of people play the ad-lib game with &quot;[redacted]&quot;?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's a silly way to make a point. If you can fit mad-lib bullshit in place of a redaction without it seeming out of place, it's usually a terribad one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is just a pretty intimidating thing to step into a community where everyone is already so integrated with one another and that presents a continuingly more inflexible set of criteria to uphold. And that was about three years ago.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Three years ago? Yeah, scratch that. I was there then, and chat *was* the best place to get critique. You just need to know how to approach. And the criteria aren't any less flexible than they were when I joined in 2011, in fact, more so if anything.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While that may have been a personal experience, it's still an experience that a response like &quot;Yeah, no.&quot; won't quite dispell.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It won't&#8230; but that's not the point.<br />
See, the people who I have seen genuinely &quot;tarred and feathered&quot; usually (read every time I can recall) did stupid things to warrant it. I can hardly speak for your case as I don't remember you and don't have logs on your case, but the usual reason people got ripped and laughed at was coming in with stuff that was faulty, in manners that were preventible if only they had bothered to read and apply the guides the sit had already, instead of wasting everyone's time.</p>

