<p>Eh, this looks alright. I for one agree with the idea that critique should be more&#8230;substantive beyond the &quot;this sucks&quot; posts. I will say however that those posts have their place, <em>sort of</em>, like how the grime sliding down the side of an unwashed garbage can has its place just to give you the indication that it's a filthy fucking garbage can that needs to be cleaned.</p>
<p>Also on the memes, if the article itself isn't really substantive to begin with, the meme would be a proper way of explaining that their article is bad. Like I have literally seen, as may of you surely have, articles that consist of 2-5 sentences, tops. Sure, we could explain in detail sometimes what we mean as some memes aren't so self explanatory.</p>
<p>On nit picking, I understand if the article is just conceptually bad and folk are just ignoring that to focus on the 2 dimensional cube the thing is locked in, but you can't simply ask people who critique literary works to simply not just pick nits. Maybe that's all they want to deal with. I do understand however if the response is disproportionally nit pickery.</p>
<p>Finally, and this is a real hard line to draw, but It's becoming a chore to decide if what I am saying is &quot;offensive&quot;. It's not as simple as being dry and detached from the comments I am placing, no. As shown above, I like to use metaphor sometimes to describe how awful I think certain aspects are and some people seem to take issue with them because they view them as insults even though they aren't intended as such.</p>

