<p>Hmm. Klink, Klink, if only you wrote some stuff yourself, I could make you my designated replacement and retire in peace :P</p>
<p>What can I say, there's some things that are meh here, but for most, you're pretty right.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Eh, this looks alright. I for one agree with the idea that critique should be more…substantive beyond the &quot;this sucks&quot; posts. I will say however that those posts have their place, sort of, like how the grime sliding down the side of an unwashed garbage can has its place just to give you the indication that it's a filthy fucking garbage can that needs to be cleaned.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, this is spot on. Calling something bad when it's an atrocity won't do, because bad is a scale. Wrongness has degrees - saying Ludolph's Number is 3.2 is wrong, but it's a damn less wrong than saying it's the one where you put your legs around the dude's neck.<br />
When someone writes an atrocious article, calling it merely bad doesn't fit the problem, and it feels wrong towards the people who wrote genuinely bad articles - ones that had effort put into them and the craft was OK, just they didn't work. Negative reinforcement works, it's been shown to a hundred of times - and if some people can't stand being told their shit doesn't in fact smell of chamomiles when they crap in the middle of the living room, then well, I won't miss them when they find a different living room to carry defecation out in.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also on the memes, if the article itself isn't really substantive to begin with, the meme would be a proper way of explaining that their article is bad. Like I have literally seen, as may of you surely have, articles that consist of 2-5 sentences, tops. Sure, we could explain in detail sometimes what we mean as some memes aren't so self explanatory.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reason I'm responding, this is totally spot on and I second it.<br />
To sum up, when the faults with your article can be summed up in one sentence, and it's been repeated so often it's a meme, then I'm sorry, but you hardly deserve anything else unless you ask very nicely. It's outright wrong to demand from people to step out of their way in fixing your mistakes when you weren't arsed to avoid the completely preventible ones in the first place.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On nit picking, I understand if the article is just conceptually bad and folk are just ignoring that to focus on the 2 dimensional cube the thing is locked in, but you can't simply ask people who critique literary works to simply not just pick nits. Maybe that's all they want to deal with. I do understand however if the response is disproportionally nit pickery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm tempted to side with Kalinin on this one, as I mentioned above with the roast pig metaphor (can you tell I was hungry?)</p>

