			<div class="content" id="post-content-2228997">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Eh, this looks alright. I for one agree with the idea that critique should be more…substantive beyond the &quot;this sucks&quot; posts. I will say however that those posts have their place, sort of, like how the grime sliding down the side of an unwashed garbage can has its place just to give you the indication that it's a filthy fucking garbage can that needs to be cleaned.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can't think of a single instance where I clicked on an article, saw a negative rating, and learned something new by reading comments of a &quot;lol this sucks&quot; nature. I'd say the negative rating conveys quite enough on its own in these cases. Also, members should be reading the articles instead of looking for shortcuts and indicators.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also on the memes, if the article itself isn't really substantive to begin with, the meme would be a proper way of explaining that their article is bad. Like I have literally seen, as may of you surely have, articles that consist of 2-5 sentences, tops. Sure, we could explain in detail sometimes what we mean as some memes aren't so self explanatory.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the article isn't substantive and lacks necessary detail, you can say that. Getting cute with cut-and-paste memes is not only lazy, but it reinforces things that are later taken as absolute truisms and become articles of dogma, whether or not they make sense.</p>
<p>The worst offender is the &quot;Series I&quot; comparison, which is completely meaningless and has become a shorthand for &quot;this is a bad article.&quot; This has been repeated <em>ad nauseum</em>, to the point where everyone's absorbed this notion that an article needs four interviews, a recovery log, a note from the skipmaker and three supplement pages before it's any good.</p>
<p>The only real purpose memes and catchphrases in critique serve is to allow for people to get in on a bad article blanket party with the least amount of effort possible.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>but you can't simply ask people who critique literary works to simply not just pick nits.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure I can. I just did. If you are incapable of assessing the overall quality of a piece, you have no business critiquing, because you have no goddamn clue what the relative importance of certain details are or what possible contextual explanations there may be. The people who know enough to cover those things will also know enough to dispense proofreading and metric system advice. Maybe this becomes appropriate once the piece is close to being posted; nine of ten times though it's people missing the forest for the trees and wasting time tinkering on the minutiae of an article that's destined to fail for more fundamental reasons.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Finally, and this is a real hard line to draw, but It's becoming a chore to decide if what I am saying is &quot;offensive&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Huh. Okay then. You're still expected to not act like a dick.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's not as simple as being dry and detached from the comments I am placing, no. As shown above, I like to use metaphor sometimes to describe how awful I think certain aspects are and some people seem to take issue with them because they view them as insults even though they aren't intended as such.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The degree to which you think things are awful aren't really relevant to a critique. You point out what works, what doesn't work, and why it doesn't work. If the only reason you can cite why something doesn't work is some overly dramatic personal narrative of how offended you are by something's badness, I'd prefer you just downvote and move along.</p>

							</div>

								
					
