<blockquote>
<p>Sure I can. I just did. If you are incapable of assessing the overall quality of a piece, you have no business critiquing, because you have no goddamn clue what the relative importance of certain details are or what possible contextual explanations there may be.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You misunderstand me, if there is a flying funny man what has ice powers and makes all the ladies swoon on the article, I understand that it's bad just to blow that off and complain that the containment cell is the improper dimensions. I do however think nitpicking always has its place somewhere. If you don't want people to nit pick, don't leave nits to pick.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The degree to which you think things are awful aren't really relevant to a critique. You point out what works, what doesn't work, and why it doesn't work. If the only reason you can cite why something doesn't work is some overly dramatic personal narrative of how offended you are by something's badness, I'd prefer you just downvote and move along.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh it does sometimes because I have seen people who just can't get it straight. I don't stoop over my keyboard and spin vitriol into a snide way of saying &quot;your article is ass, kill yourself IRL.&quot; I want to convey how bad an article is or how strange it is presented. That's what the fumbling over trying not to sound like a jerk is for; I don't want to offend. Also isn't critique expressing opinion of a certain work?</p>
<p>We can't be as detached as we want the articles to be in the series, rattling off a list of problems the author has with the article like a bunch of English teachers. We need <em>some</em> feeling behind it, else it becomes a job or a chore to talk to these people. I'm not saying we should channel our distastes and stomp the dude or have our statements dripping with contempt for somebody's work; however, we do need <em>something</em>.</p>
<p>Also on the side, VAE, I don't have the grapes for either of that. the reason I don't write here is because I don't meet the standard that this site requires. All the things I can see coming a mile away suddenly become invisible to me when it's me doing the writing with an article.</p>

