					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/madcatusa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1260716); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1260716&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="MadCatUSA" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1260716)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/madcatusa" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1260716); return false;" >MadCatUSA</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423523425 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Feb 2015 23:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229106">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Every time someone nitpicks the room dimensions while ignoring the basic unworkability of a central concept, every time someone instructs a writer to add a recovery log and three interviews to a Slenderman ripoff, every time someone offers nothing but &quot;read Series II and III&quot; as a form of critique, they are doing a disservice to the writer and the community.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do <em>some</em> of these things when I critique an article (especially room dimensions), but to be fair, I also attempt to put an ending on the critique that explains my reasoning for <em>why</em> I feel that something needs to be improved. If I'm off on my critiques, then I expect to be called on it. I have been called on them before by Zyn and others and I've worked to become better.</p>
<p>My overall feeling is that while I have been learning (and continue to learn) to be a better writer here, I'm using the critiques that I give others as a way to hone my own skills. I feel that giving a <em>meaningful</em> critique helps me to be able to pick my own work apart and view it more dispassionately, to see the logical errors that I may be committing and work to correct them.</p>
<p>My hope is that by offering critiques I can help others be a better writer and thus we both improve.</p>
<p>Now, speaking to my method of critiquing&#8230; I don't read the entire article before I start. I don't. I start at line one, assess it for what it is and move on. Generally, I have a good idea of how the article is going to go by the time I get to the end of the Containment Procedures. I use a collapsible to keep the text wall from hitting people in the face and put a paragraph after the collapsible to give my final thoughts. I've read some really well done articles that had good Containment Procedures but went nowhere. Those are the articles where I try to put something in the paragraph after the collapsible to point out that perhaps the concept needs to be re-worked.</p>

							</div>

								
					
