    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2229139">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2229139">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2229139)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2229139">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1267048&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="Ihp" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1267048)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;" >Ihp</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423527042 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Feb 2015 00:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229139">
									

<p>I have a <em>big</em> problem with this essay that can be summed up in this one line:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When you critique, you must be dispassionate and objective.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Dispassionate? Entirely possible. Objective? I feel that objective critique is impossible, unless you're solely analyzing things like word composition and use, or meter in poetry, or grammar. Sometimes, people don't like young-adult fantasy books like <em>The Hunger Games</em> and <em>Harry Potter</em>; that's subjective. Some people don't like poetry in any form; that's subjective. Some people think that Groups of Interest like the Shark Punching Center or Dr. Wondertainment or any other semi-silly GOI (such as the interpretation of Prometheus Laboratories being similar to Aperture Science from Portal) have no place on the site; that's entirely subjective.</p>
<p>The main problem I see with this overall is that it seems to think that critique of works on the site should be like writing a critique in the style of Derrida or Nietzsche, something that would appear in a 2000-page textbook for a literary criticism class. Here's the thing: by and large, the writers on the wiki are not professionals (no offense meant to anyone), and as such, we base our critique off of the most common form of criticism present today: <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reader-response_criticism">Reader-response Theory</a> (apologies for the Wikipedia link, but it's the quickest way to describe it).</p>
<p>Reader-response theory looks at&#8230;. well, the reaction a reader has to a work. For example, did reading <em>Harry Potter</em> make you sad when a character died? Did <em>Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of the Crystal Skull</em> make you laugh at its stupidity, or make you boo at the screen? Did playing the latest <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> game make you happy? All of these viewpoints are perfectly acceptable under reader-response. Most critics today use this in practice; video game reviewers, film and book critics, internet reviewers such as the Nostalgia Critic, etc.</p>
<p>The reaction a reader has to the work is the form of criticism used most often on the site, and I feel like this essay is essentially slapping everyone on the knuckles with a ruler and saying &quot;No, you're doing it wrong.&quot;</p>
<p>There's another big sticking point for me as well, and it's the first point on the bottom half of the page:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>The critic has a duty to help the writer improve.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is true. However, the form of critique outlined here only serves to <em>discourage</em> the writer, and a writer that is discouraged does not want to improve. Cold, objective, passionless critique, not one ounce of praise given, only pointing out things that need improvement. The closest thing I can think of to the &quot;dispassionate and objective&quot; critique mentioned above is what an average teacher does when they hand back a graded midterm paper. They point out places where you could use improvement(unless you're a freakish straight-A student, in which case, more power to you), and they criticize it, but they also <em>praise</em> it. They emphasize the strong points you have, point out strengths in your writing style, maybe even give you a bloody smiley-face sticker. In the end, unless they are a <em>really</em> bad teacher, they try to encourage you, and by encouraging you, you start to improve.</p>
<p>I don't terribly like this essay. That said, it does have its strong points. We have been treating our newbies badly when it comes to critique, and I do hope this at least contributes to stopping the whole &quot;Like slenderman but&quot; meme or whatever other thing is popular right now in criticism on the site. By the same token, we shouldn't be afraid to critique well-established works by well-established, well-liked authors, so this essay gets points for saying that as well.</p>
<p>That said, I disagree with this more than I agree with it, so, in the end: downvoted.</p>

							</div>

								
					
