    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2229232">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2229232">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2229232)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2229232">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1423536922 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Feb 2015 02:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229232">
									

<p>Before I get into your post, this discussion raises an interesting point. What am I calling for here, exactly? My closing statement essentially is a request for people to take criticism more seriously. How they choose to do that can vary. I offered my personal precepts for criticism as a starting point for discussion. In that, I believe this has been a successful venture. If more people approach the job of criticism earnestly and with care, then I consider this essay well worth my time. My intent is not to force everyone to be exactly like me when they critique. So I thank you for helping me to elucidate that point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I feel that objective critique is impossible</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the sense that nothing we ever do can be objective because we are human, yeah, that's probably correct. However, I disagree strongly. I believe it quite possible to step outside one's immediate perspective and assess the literary value of a piece independent of personal preference. You don't have to go far for examples. I have seen Djoric do that very thing on <a href="/scp-2188">SCP-2188</a>.</p>
<p>I personally try to step outside my own preferences when possible. Admittedly, this is something that I probably don't live up to as much as I'd like to. Certain things are entirely unacceptable to me based on reading experience, however those preferences are all traceable to logical argument if I go far back enough. I rarely go that far in the context of a single article, which is part of why I decided to start this. All this said, there are plenty of things on the site that I have no-voted or even upvoted that I did not enjoy reading. Am I an entirely self-consistent paragon of the objective viewpoint? No, and I freely admit that. But I don't see that viewpoint as inherently impossible. And I hold it as something to aspire to in the name of promoting good writing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the form of critique outlined here only serves to discourage the writer, and a writer that is discouraged does not want to improve. Cold, objective, passionless critique, not one ounce of praise given, only pointing out things that need improvement</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Mmm, no. Nothing in the system I laid out actively prevents citing the positive aspects of an article. What I wrote is geared towards fixing what's wrong, but there's no structural exclusion of praise.</p>
<p>Personally, I've never believed in the necessity of &quot;balancing out&quot; critique with praise. The writer's level of encouragement is within them. My initial precepts are that the writer has sufficient motivation and belief in their artistic vision that they are capable of hearing truths about their work, or rebutting statements about their work that are untrue. Ultimately, what's important here is the quality of the work. The site is not about stroking egos or validating someone's efforts independent of the quality of what they have written. Some people may choose to look specifically for things that they liked and single that part of the experience out. That's fine. I personally don't believe in artificially characterizing my view of a piece by engaging in that practice.</p>
<p>Also, contrary to popular belief, I do praise articles that I like. Everyone is free to take issue with the content of what I write about anything, but I reject the notion that somehow the principles I discussed in the essay somehow fundamentally prevent positive feedback. That's demonstrably false.</p>
<p>EDIT: This was meant to be a reply to ihpkmn's original post, not Klink. God damn it.</p>

							</div>

								
