			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229257">
									

<p>That'll be 30 days in the cooler.</p>
<p>I don't think encouragement has to be stroking egos or pumping somebody up, it could be as simple as the minimalistic and oft used, &quot;has potential&quot; or asking the writer to keep at it or some such weakly positive statements. Statements that while admittedly are weaksauce and can seem patronizing, actually can go a long way and can make the difference in somebody determining if they can have a place here.</p>
<p>However, if they can't take the heat, even a little bit, I believe it shouldn't be our problem to try and pump them up.</p>
<p>Also, leaving your bubble and judging people's work plainly <em>is</em> important, but you can't divorce yourself from <em>your</em> feelings and <em>your opinion</em>. If I did that, then it would just feel like I am sitting around, grading people's creative lit homework, making sure their work is free of conceptual errors and crimes against the english language. I and many others here I'm sure, critique based on how they feel about something as there is no absolutely <em>right</em> way to write something. I am not accusing you of opposing this completely; the vibe of your statements on this matter just give me this feeling.</p>

							</div>

								
					
