					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1267048&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="Ihp" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1267048)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ihp" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1267048); return false;" >Ihp</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423539629 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Feb 2015 03:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229276">
									

<p>I'll consent to you on 80% of the points. This one, however, I cannot abide by:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The writer's level of encouragement is within them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No writer is an independent creature. Hell, nobody who's ever done a creative work <em>period</em> is independent (the title &quot;indie&quot; given to certain music, film and video game creators notwithstanding). Everyone gets discouraged by something, whether it be the bills being past due or the fact that you did badly on your last exam or the fact that your relationship with your significant other is undergoing some hardship. Some people may be able to motivate themselves to do great things, but that's rarer than finding someone who liked Birdemic unironically.</p>
<p>If I may, let me quote something by Kevin Smith. (Yes, <em>that</em> Kevin Smith, the guy behind effing Clerks):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Remember: It costs nothing to encourage an artist, and the potential benefits are staggering. A pat on the back to an artist now could one day result in your favorite film, or the cartoon you love to get stoned watching, or the song that saves your life. Discourage an artist, you get absolutely nothing in return, ever.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sometimes, a writer or a painter or a bloody director needs a pat on the back to go on. Encouragement isn't internal for most people, and certainly not for most writers. I know or know of a few people on the wiki that have needed that little bit of encouragement to get them to finish an SCP they weren't sure if it should go up, or to get a tale finally published despite the fact that almost nobody reads tales, or to enter a contest they probably won't win.</p>
<p>And to Klink's point of &quot;If you can't take the heat, get out of the <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">tired metaphor and think of a better one</span> kitchen&quot;, sometimes, you <em>can't</em> get out of the kitchen, as hokey as that may sound. Any creative community is going to be full of bad critics and trolls and people who outright <em>hate</em> your work, unless you are <em>very</em> lucky or <em>very</em> obscure. But at the same time, for quite a few of us, writing is what we do because we love it.</p>
<p>Going back to the kitchen metaphor: some of us can't take the heat, and we get burned. But after a bit, some of us cool off, get right back in there, and start cooking again. And sometimes, we can't bring ourselves to start creating again after being burned until someone gives us a pat on the back, an encouraging smile, and says &quot;go at it&quot;.</p>
<p>If you think a writer should make their own encouragement? Fine. But I'm willing to bet that there are quite a few people who disagree with that sentiment. In fact, I know there are; they just haven't said anything yet.</p>

							</div>

								
					
