				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vaelynx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(914331); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=914331&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="VAElynx" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=914331)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vaelynx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(914331); return false;" >VAElynx</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423550082 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Feb 2015 06:34</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229401">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Sometimes, a writer or a painter or a bloody director needs a pat on the back to go on. Encouragement isn't internal for most people, and certainly not for most writers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Except, you know. A critic's job isn't to provide encouragement. It's to criticize a piece.<br />
That's not to say they *can't* do it if they deem it appropriate, but they aren't obliged to.<br />
Not the least because a site critic acts for the benefit of the site, not any particular would-be-author. Your &quot;job&quot; is to make sure quality articles get written, but it's irrelevant who they get written by.</p>
<p>I mean, the HR department at a company decides whether they hire you or not as well, but I haven't seen them providing pat-back quotes and &quot;I'm sure you'll do well on your next interview.&quot; either.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Remember: It costs nothing to encourage an artist, and the potential benefits are staggering. A pat on the back to an artist now could one day result in your favorite film, or the cartoon you love to get stoned watching, or the song that saves your life. Discourage an artist, you get absolutely nothing in return, ever.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, no. This is essentially the anti-abortion argument that says Beethoven would have been aborted, rehashed.<br />
Sure, you might have the next Sorts or Aelanna on hand, but, if their output is atrocious now, it's distinctively unlikely. If they'll try their best to better themselves and eventually start writing well&#8230; more power to them, sure. But, again, you aren't, and sure as hell shouldn't be obliged to lead them there by the hand.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I know or know of a few people on the wiki that have needed that little bit of encouragement to get them to finish an SCP they weren't sure if it should go up, or to get a tale finally published despite the fact that almost nobody reads tales, or to enter a contest they probably won't win.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hey, guess what. Given it's the story of my highest-rated article (001-EX-J), I'm one of those people.</p>
<p>The problem here is what you don't say. That the encouragement stemmed from a genuine attitude about the article in progress.</p>
<p>Now, suppose my feelings were right, what I had was atrocious, and someone still encouraged me to post it because they were nice. What would happen is obvious. It'd tank.</p>
<p>Now, there's two potential results here. Either I'd be inclined to trust the person less when I asked them for opinion next, and favour the inner voice telling me it's crap instead. Which could make the scenario repeat the next time (because really, you have no way of expressing approval beyond expressing approval) even though that time, the potential and whatnot there might be genuine.</p>
<p>Or, of course, I keep on trusting them, and write more atrocious crap. You know, I think the world has enough chrischans, tara gillesbies and parkourdude91s without someone else to add to the numbers of graphomaniacs producing stuff nobody wants to read except for quiet amusement at the author's expense. Ever heard of the Maradonia Saga? The author is apparently still persuaded she's one of the world's youngest successful novelists, and the fault lies almost entirely in those surrounding her.</p>
<p>And, you know, this kinda reveals the biggest problem with your sort of approach.<br />
When you use praise as a platitude, it degenerates into that, instead of a genuine indicator of (subjective) value. You devalue the highest currency a working for free author can get into something dumped over critique to make it easier to stomach, but in itself meaning precisely nothing. When you tell someone what they do has potential, when it really doesn't, and they believe you, you have essentially wasted their time - time that will get put in into doing something that has little chance of success, instead of something they might actually be good at.</p>
<p>Plus, this <a href="http://cdn.twentytwowords.com/wp-content/uploads/Tell-Youre-Kid-Theyre-Not-Special-634x1091.png?564e0c">Cyanide and Happiness comic</a> summarizes my attitude perhaps better than I could.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And to Klink's point of &quot;If you can't take the heat, get out of the tired metaphor and think of a better one kitchen&quot;, sometimes, you can't get out of the kitchen, as hokey as that may sound. Any creative community is going to be full of bad critics and trolls and people who outright hate your work, unless you are very lucky or very obscure. But at the same time, for quite a few of us, writing is what we do because we love it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Except, you know. You aren't obliged to stay in a particular creative community if you don't want to, and they aren't obliged to try to keep you in.<br />
That's what Klink's quote referred to, and I stand by it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Going back to the kitchen metaphor: some of us can't take the heat, and we get burned. But after a bit, some of us cool off, get right back in there, and start cooking again. And sometimes, we can't bring ourselves to start creating again after being burned until someone gives us a pat on the back, an encouraging smile, and says &quot;go at it&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And again - starting to create/cook again has no inherent value for anyone, not even yourself. In fact, to extend the metaphor, if you're an atrocious cook, encouraging you is begging for food poisoning at worst, and a horrid meal at best.</p>

							</div>

								
					
