<blockquote>
<p>You should just point out the good in an article so that they don't feel like they can't do this and so they know what to do the same should they choose to rewrite or something like that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;so that they don't feel like they can't do this&quot; is an expression of the entire thing I have been arguing against. If someone shows all the indicators that they indeed &quot;can't do this&quot; there's no use in trying to make them feel the opposite - in fact, it's bad for everyone involved.</p>
<p>If you see a way a concept can be salvaged? Sure, point it out. Making an article work is really one of the most pleasant aspects of critique there is. However, there's no use to giving out empty platitudes as participation awards. &quot;You didn't fuck up grammar&quot; isn't a reason for praise, much as knowing how to take apart and assemble a gun isn't reason to get a medal in the army.</p>

							</div>

								
					
