<p>I'm using <em>an analogy</em>.</p>
<p>See, the two arguments are flawed for exactly the same reason.<br />
They put forth that sinking investment into something that has all the indicators of failure is a good thing because there's a <em>possibility</em> a success will result, counter to the evidence, neglecting both the low probability involved (and indeed, the higher probability of success investing the same resources into something that will work) and the negative consequences to, well, investing resources into something full of failure indicators.</p>

