				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=732274&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="thedeadlymoose" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=732274)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;" >thedeadlymoose</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423539531 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Feb 2015 03:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2229274">
									

<p>Heh. I actually missed the line that you quote here, &#8230;and I agree it's a bad one. There is no obligation to be dispassionate, and &quot;objective&quot; is rarely possible. Reader-response critique is valid.</p>
<p>Didn't see this as key to the essay, though. It's a bit of a silly meme in and of itself, and could be easily cut.</p>
<p>I start to diverge from you in your second sticking point, though:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, the form of critique outlined here only serves to discourage the writer, and a writer that is discouraged does not want to improve.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not necessarily. It can often encourage writers. And frankly ANY negative critique can seriously upset people; we're not culturally inclined to take feedback well. Even in a professional workshop environment you see people get upset by critique.</p>
<p>Unfortunately there is no way around this: none.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Cold, objective, passionless critique, not one ounce of praise given, only pointing out things that need improvement.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Pretty much everything you're extrapolating here isn't actually in the essay. No one said anything about &quot;no praise&quot;.</p>
<p>Also, we are not teachers. We are not obligated to encourage. And not a single person on this site always encourages, which makes this&#8230; a touch weird.</p>
<p>It IS an oft-missed point that good critique should mention Things The Reader Liked, which is often harder to remember but just as vital as pointing out problems. Otherwise, those Things can be lost in the next edit, or devalued by the author and not show up again (instead replaced by adherence to critique memes and blending in with the wallpaper as much as possible!).</p>
<p>But then, we presently have a lot of problems with people praising something merely for existing, or for existing and not being actively terrible and not violating Writing Taboos. We have <em>so many</em> mediocre, forgettable pieces upvoted because they're not actively terrible, violate no taboos, and the author is reasonably well-liked (or simply not disliked). That isn't good for anyone, neither the authors nor the readers.</p>
<p>EDIT: Argh, oops. I hadn't seen that Kalinin replied already, covering some of this stuff. Apologies for the redundancies, where they exist.</p>

							</div>

								
